WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
Node.

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.428
js is a common platform for APIs, web applications, microservices, automation,

00:00:05.428 --> 00:00:07.555
serverless functions, and backend-for-frontend systems.

00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:12.233
It often sits close to users, data, identity, payments, internal services,

00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:13.509
and third-party APIs.

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:14.785
That makes Node.

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:18.613
js security a practical concern for developers and operators,

00:00:18.613 --> 00:00:24.567
not a specialty topic that can be pushed to the end of the project.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:28.394
Running JavaScript on the server changes the security picture.

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:33.073
The application may parse untrusted requests, read environment variables, call databases,

00:00:33.073 --> 00:00:37.326
process files, launch background work, and talk to other services.

00:00:37.326 --> 00:00:40.303
Asynchronous execution and event-driven architecture help Node.

00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:45.832
js handle many concurrent operations, but they also require careful thinking about errors,

00:00:45.832 --> 00:00:49.234
shared state, timeouts, resource limits, and operational behavior.

00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:49.884
Node.

00:00:49.884 --> 00:00:52.436
js development is package-heavy by design.

00:00:52.436 --> 00:00:57.539
Teams can move quickly because npm gives access to a large ecosystem,

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:01.792
but every dependency becomes part of the application's trust boundary.

00:01:01.792 --> 00:01:04.344
Application security, runtime security, dependency security,

00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:08.172
and deployment security all meet in the same service.

00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:14.126
A safe route handler can still be harmed by weak secrets management, vulnerable middleware,

00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:17.103
unsafe package scripts, or noisy production errors.

00:01:17.103 --> 00:01:22.207
The goal is reliable software that handles untrusted input, secrets, users, files,

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:23.483
and dependencies safely.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.885
That does not mean memorizing every possible vulnerability.

00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:30.288
It means designing explicit boundaries, validating runtime data,

00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:34.541
enforcing authorization on the server, managing dependencies deliberately, protecting secrets,

00:01:34.541 --> 00:01:39.620
and making production behavior observable without turning the application into a data leak.
